  "result": "I opened the issue: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13. I didn't change any code.\n\nI ran the function to confirm the bug: `word_count(\"\")` returns 1. The cause is in `textstats/__init__.py:6`, which uses `text.split(\" \")`. Splitting an empty string that way gives `[\"\"]`, a list with one empty item, so the count is 1.\n\nThe same split also miscounts two other kinds of input, and the issue covers both:\n- **Whitespace only:** `\"   \"` returns 4.\n- **Repeated spaces:** `\"a  b\"` returns 3.\n\nThe issue suggests using `text.split()` with no argument, which is what `average_word_length` already does. It also suggests adding a test for the empty string.\n\nThe git remote is a local path, but `gh` linked this checkout to `acme/textstats`, so the issue went there. If that's the wrong repo, tell me and I'll close it.",
